        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "Rationalizing Opacity",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                117,
                370,
                149
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 2,
            "global_block_id": 2,
            "global_group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    117.0
                ],
                [
                    370.0,
                    117.0
                ],
                [
                    370.0,
                    149.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    149.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Some writers, believe it or not, are skeptical of clarity. How should we respond to those who know they write in a complex style but claim they must because they are breaking new intellectual ground, or those who frame clear writing itself as a disservice to readers? Are they right, or are these self-serving rationalizations? These are vexing questions that can be settled only case by case—and even then not to everyone's satisfaction.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                161,
                785,
                316
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 3,
            "global_block_id": 3,
            "global_group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    161.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    161.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    316.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    316.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "Necessary Complexity",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                335,
                339,
                364
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 4,
            "global_block_id": 4,
            "global_group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    335.0
                ],
                [
                    339.0,
                    335.0
                ],
                [
                    339.0,
                    364.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    364.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Here, for example, is a sentence from a leading figure in contemporary literary theory:",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                371,
                782,
                422
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 5,
            "global_block_id": 5,
            "global_group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    371.0
                ],
                [
                    782.0,
                    371.0
                ],
                [
                    782.0,
                    422.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    422.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "If, for a while, the ruse of desire is calculable for the uses of discipline soon the repetition of guilt, justification, pseudo-scientific theories, superstition, spurious authorities, and classifications can be seen as the desperate effort to “normalize” formally the disturbance of a discourse of splitting that violates the rational, enlightened claims of its enunciatory modality.",
            "block_bbox": [
                125,
                434,
                757,
                559
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 6,
            "global_block_id": 6,
            "global_group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    125.0,
                    434.0
                ],
                [
                    757.0,
                    434.0
                ],
                [
                    757.0,
                    559.0
                ],
                [
                    125.0,
                    559.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "—Homi Bhabha, “Of Mimicry and Man: The Ambivalence of Colonial Discourse”",
            "block_bbox": [
                162,
                572,
                786,
                596
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 7,
            "global_block_id": 7,
            "global_group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    162.0,
                    572.0
                ],
                [
                    786.0,
                    572.0
                ],
                [
                    786.0,
                    596.0
                ],
                [
                    162.0,
                    596.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Does that sentence express a thought so subtle and complex that its substance can be expressed only as written? Or is it academic babble? How do we decide whether in fact his nuances are, at least for ordinarily competent readers, just not accessible given the time most of us have for figuring them out?",
            "block_bbox": [
                91,
                610,
                784,
                712
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 8,
            "global_block_id": 8,
            "global_group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    91.0,
                    610.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    610.0
                ],
                [
                    784.0,
                    712.0
                ],
                [
                    91.0,
                    712.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "We owe our readers precise and nuanced prose, but we ought not to assume that they owe us an inordinate amount of their time to unpack it. If we deliberately write in ways that we know will make readers struggle—well, that's a gambit we choose to play. In the marketplace of ideas, truth is the prime value but not the only one. Another is what it costs us to find it.",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                715,
                785,
                841
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 9,
            "global_block_id": 9,
            "global_group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    715.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    715.0
                ],
                [
                    785.0,
                    841.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    841.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The fact is, when writers claim their prose style must be difficult because their ideas are so complex or new, they are more often wrong than right. The philosopher of language Ludwig Wittgenstein said:",
            "block_bbox": [
                90,
                844,
                783,
                922
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 10,
            "global_block_id": 10,
            "global_group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    90.0,
                    844.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    844.0
                ],
                [
                    783.0,
                    922.0
                ],
                [
                    90.0,
                    922.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Whatever can be thought can be thought clearly; whatever can be written can be written clearly.",
            "block_bbox": [
                125,
                933,
                753,
                981
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 10,
            "group_id": 11,
            "global_block_id": 11,
            "global_group_id": 11,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    125.0,
                    933.0
                ],
                [
                    753.0,
                    933.0
                ],
                [
                    753.0,
                    981.0
                ],
                [
                    125.0,
                    981.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "We'll add a nuance:",
            "block_bbox": [
                92,
                997,
                264,
                1020
            ],
            "block_id": 12,
            "block_order": 11,
            "group_id": 12,
            "global_block_id": 12,
            "global_group_id": 12,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    92.0,
                    997.0
                ],
                [
                    264.0,
                    997.0
                ],
                [
                    264.0,
                    1020.0
                ],
                [
                    92.0,
                    1020.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "...and with just a bit more effort, more clearly still.",
            "block_bbox": [
                122,
                1034,
                517,
                1058
            ],
            "block_id": 13,
            "block_order": 12,
            "group_id": 13,
            "global_block_id": 13,
            "global_group_id": 13,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
